      "text": "The maintenance sweep walks every registered domain and returns a ranked work queue. It detects temporal debt such as an elapsed validity window on knowledge still marked stable, a review date that has come and gone, and knowledge nobody has verified in a long time. It detects structural gaps too: unresolved links, one sided relation pairs, orphans with no inbound or outbound edges, oversized pages that ought to be split, and near empty stubs that carry no content at all. Redundancy is the third family: clusters of near duplicate text, pairs that say the same thing in different words, and tags that have drifted away from the controlled vocabulary. Nothing is changed by the sweep itself; every finding names the page, the evidence it fired on, and the exact next action together with the tool that performs it. A finding marked mechanical completes intent the archive already records, while a finding marked judgment changes what the archive claims and needs a human yes before anything is written. The queue is paged, can be narrowed to one family or one rule, and can be evaluated as of a fixed date so a run is reproducible. Work the queue with the write tools and run the same scope again to confirm that it shrank. This paragraph exists only to give the parity comparison a long input of roughly four hundred tokens, so that the local attention window of one hundred and twenty eight positions is genuinely exercised on both sides of the comparison rather than only the short sentences above it. Local attention means each position attends to a band of sixty four positions on either side, and only every third layer sees the whole sequence, so a short sentence never distinguishes a correct sliding window from a broken one. The text keeps going for a while longer for exactly that reason, and mentions dates, numbers and punctuation so the tokenizer has something other than plain lowercase words to chew on.",
